# 14 What the outlet data gives Castrol (new)
"""[17:30 - 20:00]  KEY MESSAGE: the same field data answers the questions sales and marketing teams ask every month.
So what does all this give Castrol's sales and marketing teams? Six things.
One, distribution gaps: filter the outlets without Castrol on the shelf, by region and district. That is your list for distribution drives.
Two, fake product hotspots: every outlet where fake product was seen, with the visit date and the photo. That is evidence for action, not a rumour.
Three, competitor presence: which brands each outlet carries, recorded at every visit, not once a year.
Four, the outlets to win next: potential customers with their level, the reason they do not buy, and the litres we expect each month. You can see where a price or a contract is the barrier.
Five, order rhythm: each outlet's usual basket and how often it orders. When an outlet stops ordering, the gap becomes a task for the rep, as you will see in chapter four.
And six, campaign follow-up: because the shelf is checked at every visit, you can compare the shelf before and after a campaign, outlet by outlet.
Go back to the campaign from the beginning. With KAD OS the marketing team does not wait two weeks. The sales director opens the filters: region, shops without the product on the shelf, and the potential outlets nearby. In a minute there is a target list, and each rep sees his part of it on his route. A month later, the shelf checks show which outlets now carry the product. That is the difference between hoping and knowing.
That ends chapter two: the brand sees every outlet.""",

